WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:08.630
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:20.550
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:27.080
We are just rocking out to our own intro.

9
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.000
We were actually dancing and miming the words.

10
00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:30.900
Hello, everyone.

11
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.120
Today, we are going to be talking about The Fight.

12
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:35.340
The Fight.

13
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:43.360
Season 2, Episode 6, written by Gene Stupnitsky and Lee Eisenberg, known to us as Gene and Lee.

14
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.020
They're a duo.

15
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:45.120
They're funny fellas.

16
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.640
This episode was directed by Ken Kwapis.

17
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.320
We love Ken Kwapis.

18
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.180
We love Ken Kwapis.

19
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.580
I don't even know if it's possible for people to understand our love of Ken Kwapis.

20
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:57.720
I don't know, guys.

21
00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.040
There are some directors that just are in our hearts.

22
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.900
Ken Kwapis, Paul Feig.

23
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.140
Charles McDougal.

24
00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:05.060
Charles McDougal.

25
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.340
For yelling action at us.

26
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:07.000
For a different way, though.

27
00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.500
Like, Ken Kwapis is like a cozy blanket.

28
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.580
Paul Feig, you want to hang out with all day.

29
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:12.560
And drink martinis.

30
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:18.160
Charles McDougal, you kind of want to get drunk in a pub with and yell at people.

31
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:20.520
It's so true.

32
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:22.800
We'll talk about Charles McDougal when we get to him.

33
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.540
We haven't really had a chance to talk about him, but that was a little preview.

34
00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:26.280
Mm-hmm.

35
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.060
We have a special treat today.

36
00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.900
Our summary of this episode is going to be read by a fan.

37
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:33.900
Dun-dun-dun.

38
00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:34.920
So here we go.

39
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.400
Alexis is describing the fight.

40
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:42.980
Dwight becomes assistant to the sensei at his dojo and spends the day bragging and telling everyone how tough he is.

41
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.040
Michael has a lot of papers to sign.

42
00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.740
Jim and Pam use the opportunity to trick Michael by saying they think Dwight is stronger than Michael is.

43
00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:54.240
So Michael agrees to duel Dwight at the dojo to prove his manliness and procrastinate doing his work.

44
00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:55.620
I thought that was very good.

45
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:56.360
That was really good.

46
00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:57.340
I feel like she covered it.

47
00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:58.660
She was very concise.

48
00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.700
I feel like maybe she wrote it down and read it, which is what I would do.

49
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:02.640
So, Alexis.

50
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.640
Alexis, you've made a friend.

51
00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:07.300
That's right.

52
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:08.420
All right.

53
00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.000
Let's get into the fast facts for the fight.

54
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:17.520
Fast fact number one, this is the first episode from our new writing team of Gene and Lee.

55
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.700
I emailed with Lee Eisenberg.

56
00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:19.900
Okay.

57
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:20.140
Okay.

58
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:26.280
And he told me that before getting hired on the office, Gene was a part-time nanny.

59
00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.180
Ah!

60
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.680
And Lee was an assistant to a director.

61
00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.200
This was their first staff writing job.

62
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.480
They were over the moon, and they were particularly excited about the free lunches.

63
00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:40.720
And I can really relate to that.

64
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:41.600
I can, too.

65
00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.760
When I interned on Conan O'Brien, I got free food, and I was so excited.

66
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:52.300
Every time I got a job as an actor, when I was a struggling actor, I got excited about the free meals.

67
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.000
The free food at work.

68
00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:54.780
It's a big deal.

69
00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.400
I kind of still get excited about it, I have to be honeSt.

70
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:02.080
I mean, I hate to break it to everyone at Earwolf, but I hit up that fruit bowl hard

71
00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.620
every morning when we come here.

72
00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:04.040
Yeah.

73
00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.180
Like, apple, banana.

74
00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.300
Earwolf has a basket of chips that's really good, too.

75
00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:08.620
Yeah.

76
00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:13.840
When I worked in offices in real life, I used to get really excited to go in the break room,

77
00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.840
and, like, someone would bring in some Danish or some muffins or cookies.

78
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:19.820
I don't know.

79
00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.240
But if you were anything like me, I would eat ramen noodles because you could buy nine for a dollar.

80
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:25.320
Oh, yeah.

81
00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:25.920
Oh, man.

82
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:30.680
You know, ramen noodles are really a great meal for, like, family meals with kids.

83
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.120
You can spruce it up.

84
00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:37.040
You can sauté some brown chicken and put a bunch of veggies in with the noodles.

85
00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:37.540
Mm-hmm.

86
00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:43.780
You can also not cook them at all and crunch them up and put them in a salad instead of a

87
00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:44.220
crouton.

88
00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.480
This has turned into a different show.

89
00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:47.020
Okay.

90
00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.920
That's like a – you can make a Chinese chicken salad.

91
00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.860
You can use ramen noodles as your crunchies.

92
00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:52.400
Wow.

93
00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:53.260
There you go.

94
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.720
Some more information from Lee about this being his first episode.

95
00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.440
He said they were terrified because, remember, we talked about how they break down the episodes

96
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.200
as a group and then writers get assigned to write the episode.

97
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:14.180
He said that since their episode was episode six, they had very little in their outline.

98
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:19.000
He said they had fleshed out the other episodes much more than this one,

99
00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.860
and he thought that they were being set up to fail.

100
00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.200
And they were being tested.

101
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.700
Yes, exactly, because he was like, how do you give the staff writers with the least amount

102
00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.400
of experience the least worked on outline?

103
00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:31.360
Yes, exactly.

104
00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.000
He said after they turned in their script and they had to get their notes on their script,

105
00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:40.500
Greg had pneumonia, and they had to go to Greg's house in order to get their notes.

106
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:46.780
And he said the thing that really stood out to him was that he explained the idea of the

107
00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:49.420
Jim-Pam subtext to Gene and Lee.

108
00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:57.220
They had written a really on-the-nose Jim-Pam scene, and Greg said, sometimes just a look

109
00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:57.920
is enough.

110
00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:04.640
And he said it kind of blew their minds and that Greg completely brought that idea to them.

111
00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:11.900
And in the episode, something I remember is when Pam is reading Jim's palm, that was a

112
00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:12.360
big deal.

113
00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:19.440
It was how, there was always this discussion, how can Jim and Pam physically touch without

114
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.140
it being inappropriate?

115
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:21.780
Right.

116
00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.920
Well, lady, I think that was very intimate.

117
00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:29.640
I even looked up online about hand touching, and it is considered physical intimacy.

118
00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:31.420
That was some major flirting.

119
00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.100
That's a note card I have for later.

120
00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:33.540
All right.

121
00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.740
Should we move on to fast fact number two?

122
00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:36.460
Let's do it.

123
00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:36.860
All right.

124
00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:41.660
Fast fact number two, Sensei Ira was played by Lance Kroll.

125
00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.040
Is it Sensei or Sensei?

126
00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:47.980
Oh my gosh.

127
00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.660
It's Sensei Ira was played by Lance Kroll, who's a friend of mine.

128
00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:56.120
And I have five note cards on him because he and I talked on the phone yesterday.

129
00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:01.140
Oh, you know that I was very starstruck by him when he was on our episode.

130
00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:01.780
I know.

131
00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.200
He even said, I think Jenna watched a lot of Joe Schmo.

132
00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:05.880
Yes.

133
00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:07.720
That cracks me up that he said that.

134
00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:13.260
Because I did corner him at lunch one day, and I made him tell me everything about the Joe Schmo show.

135
00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:16.060
If you guys have not seen this, please go look it up.

136
00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:27.080
The Joe Schmo show was a fake reality show contest where everybody on the show were actors except for one person.

137
00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:34.220
The Joe Schmo guy was a real person, and everyone around him was acting like they were in a reality show.

138
00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:40.840
Yes, and they were locked in this house, and they had to do competitions and vote each other off at the end.

139
00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:44.760
By the way, Kristen Wiig is on that show with Lance.

140
00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:48.640
A lot of great comedians and improvisers that I knew were on that show.

141
00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:49.900
I auditioned for that.

142
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:55.860
It was cast by Allison Jones, and I was devastated when I did not get it because I thought,

143
00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:02.520
what an amazing acting experience to live in a house and have to play this character 24-7.

144
00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:04.600
And by the way, the show is brilliant.

145
00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.960
So, yes, I am starstruck by Lance.

146
00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:08.120
I can't believe you know him.

147
00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.780
I can't believe I didn't know that you know him.

148
00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.820
So, I emailed him yesterday, and I was like, Lance, we're going to be talking about the fight.

149
00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.140
Where are you?

150
00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:16.540
Can you hop on the phone?

151
00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:18.500
And he was like, I'm actually in Atlanta.

152
00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.900
He has moved to Atlanta, and he set up his own production company because he's really talented.

153
00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:25.600
He's an actor, comedian, writer.

154
00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:26.100
Yeah.

155
00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:30.460
And he has a production company in Atlanta called Picture It Productions.

156
00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:32.020
How about that?

157
00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.200
You can check it out at pictureitproductions.com.

158
00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:35.260
All right, Lance.

159
00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:40.680
Lance, so we met so long ago, and I said, Lance, did we meet doing improv?

160
00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.600
I can't remember exactly how we met.

161
00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.480
And he remembered exactly, and it made me laugh so hard.

162
00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.520
Okay, so he was friends.

163
00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:53.960
We had mutual friends, and I went into an editing bay where a friend of ours worked, and they

164
00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:55.760
were helping me with my acting reel.

165
00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:56.540
Okay.

166
00:07:56.780 --> 00:07:58.580
And I said, Lance, was this before the office?

167
00:07:58.740 --> 00:07:59.820
He said, no, Angela.

168
00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.560
It was like after the pilot, but you really didn't think the show was going to go.

169
00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:10.640
So you were trying to put some of your office scene with Kevin in accounting in your reel?

170
00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:12.200
Should we say what a reel is?

171
00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:13.020
Yes, yes.

172
00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:18.020
So just for people who don't know, actors have a headshot, and on the back is their

173
00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:24.880
resume, and you give that at auditions, but you also would send a link to clips of various

174
00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:27.480
performances you had been on, and that's your acting reel.

175
00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:28.460
It helps you get jobs.

176
00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:33.620
So, Angela, you were putting together your acting reel with some clips from the office.

177
00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:38.340
And this really dates me and our show because it was all on VHS.

178
00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:39.500
Oh, my gosh.

179
00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:41.600
There was a guy actually editing it.

180
00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:45.800
Yes, and I had brought in like a box of all of my footage, and they were putting together

181
00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:47.340
a montage of my scenes.

182
00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:51.940
And Lance said, Angela, it was so cute and charming because you turned to me and you

183
00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:53.180
said, hey, will you watch this?

184
00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.820
What do you think of my reel?

185
00:08:54.820 --> 00:09:00.540
And he said, Ange, it was every second you've ever been on screen.

186
00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:03.020
He said it was so long.

187
00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.960
And also, in some of it, you didn't even have dialogue.

188
00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.280
You just walked by.

189
00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:09.020
That is hilarious.

190
00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:12.760
There was like a commercial like I did for Chrysler where I walked by and I just went,

191
00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:15.320
and I did thumbs up at someone.

192
00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:18.640
I was like, oh, my gosh, Lance, it's so embarrassing.

193
00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:20.100
But that's the first time we met.

194
00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:25.020
But so it was after the pilot when I thought the office wasn't going to go, and I was trying

195
00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:26.380
to figure out how to get more work.

196
00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:29.620
That's very funny that then he ends up being on the show.

197
00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:30.220
I know.

198
00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.060
And then he told me, he said, Ange, did you know I tested for the role of Jim?

199
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.800
He tested for Jim.

200
00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:37.300
What?

201
00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.940
So I must have done scenes with him.

202
00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:42.900
He said you weren't in his group on his test day.

203
00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:43.280
Oh, okay.

204
00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:47.960
He had a different group, and Bob Odenkirk was in his group for Michael Scott.

205
00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:48.900
Oh, yes.

206
00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:49.560
That's right.

207
00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:51.760
I was in the Steve Carell test group.

208
00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.920
Well, you know something that's funny about Bob Odenkirk since you brought him up?

209
00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:59.720
I knew Bob Odenkirk when I was auditioning for the office, and we were both called.

210
00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:02.900
I was called to do Pam, and he was called to do Michael.

211
00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.980
So we got together, and we practiced together.

212
00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:06.600
Aw.

213
00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:09.020
But then I wasn't in his test group.

214
00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:09.400
Oh.

215
00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.200
And I had thought, darn it.

216
00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.580
We worked a bunch of bits together.

217
00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.280
We were so excited to show them off.

218
00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:22.780
But he did this very funny audition where he brought in his guitar, and he sang a song to Pam, and we had worked it all out.

219
00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:26.260
We were going to ask if we could do it if we were paired together, but we never were.

220
00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:30.540
So Lance told me this great story that the day that he was screen testing, you know, he's in the lobby waiting.

221
00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:33.120
They were all kind of giddy that Bob Odenkirk was in their group.

222
00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:34.780
They were all really big fans of Bob's.

223
00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:38.460
And Bob walked over to Lance and was like, hey, you want to run lines with me?

224
00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:40.900
And Lance was like, uh, sure.

225
00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:47.400
And he said it was really cool, and they, like, sort of went out in this lobby and ran lines, like, three or four times, like, the scenes all the way through.

226
00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:49.180
He just thought it was a really cool moment.

227
00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:53.820
So Lance said when he read for Jim, he was like, you know, I really felt like I just tried too hard.

228
00:10:54.060 --> 00:11:01.700
And he said then when he got called back when we were – once the show was up and going for the fight, he read that it was, like, a martial arts guy.

229
00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:03.240
And he goes, oh, my gosh.

230
00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:05.720
Lance's background is actually martial arts.

231
00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:08.340
He was in the martial arts community for 15 years.

232
00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.200
He has a third-degree black belt in taekwondo.

233
00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:16.220
He was the sixth in the nation in his, like, weight category in 1992.

234
00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:19.400
He trained junior Olympic gold medalists.

235
00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:20.180
Like, he was –

236
00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:21.740
Did they know this about him?

237
00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:22.480
I mean, I –

238
00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:23.800
I think they didn't.

239
00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:24.520
I don't know.

240
00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:27.280
But he said when he saw who the character was, he was like, oh, my gosh.

241
00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:30.720
He said, I am not going to try to go for the joke at all.

242
00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.360
I'm just going to go in as if I'm, like, working with one of my students and teaching a class.

